  <div class="deck">
    <div class="slides" id="slides">

      <section class="slide">
        <div class="kicker reveal">arXiv:2504.04715 · LLM API Integrity</div>
        <div class="slide-content">
          <h1 class="title-xl reveal d2">Auditing Model Substitution in LLM APIs: Why Software Tests Fail and Hardware Attestation Wins</h1>
          <p class="subtitle reveal d3">Commercial APIs can silently swap to cheaper models; empirical results show software auditing breaks under realistic conditions, while TEEs offer verifiable inference.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="footer reveal d4"><span>Audience: ML researchers · AI security · cloud infra engineers</span><span>1 / 9</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="slide">
        <div class="kicker">Threat Model</div>
        <div class="slide-content">
          <h2>The Model Substitution Problem: Economic Incentives Drive Covert Swaps</h2>
          <div class="grid-2">
            <div class="card">
              <h3 class="highlight">Why substitution happens</h3>
              <ul>
                <li>APIs bill for premium models while providers optimize GPU spend.</li>
                <li>Cheaper alternatives can preserve user-perceived quality.</li>
                <li>Black-box interfaces prevent direct verification of served weights.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="card">
              <h3 class="highlight">Formal test framing</h3>
              <p class="mono">H0: P_api(y|x) = P_spec(y|x)</p>
              <p class="mono">H1: P_api(y|x) ≠ P_spec(y|x)</p>
              <p class="small">Goal: infer whether observed API outputs come from the advertised model or a substitute.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="grid-2">
            <div class="card"><strong>Substitution types</strong><br><span class="small">1) Smaller model<br>2) Quantized variant<br>3) Fine-tuned/updated checkpoint<br>4) Different model family</span></div>
            <div class="card"><span class="badge">Core challenge</span><p class="small">Auditors observe outputs only, not model internals, deployment stack, or routing policies.</p></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="footer"><span></span><span>2 / 9</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="slide">
        <div class="kicker">Empirical Evidence</div>
        <div class="slide-content">
          <h2>FP8 Quantization Preserves Most Accuracy — Making Substitution Hard to Detect</h2>
          <table class="table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Family</th><th>MMLU</th><th>GSM8K</th><th>MATH</th><th>GPQA Diamond</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Llama-3-8B</td><td>62.69 → 62.43</td><td>61.14 → 60.90</td><td>20.65 → 14.91</td><td>22.62 → 20.14</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Llama-3-70B</td><td>78.05 → 77.88</td><td>88.06 → 87.35</td><td>35.69 → 35.75</td><td>29.60 → 33.12</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Gemma-2-9B</td><td>71.86 → 71.92</td><td>81.80 → 79.41</td><td>33.41 → 32.53</td><td>28.64 → 27.81</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Qwen2-72B</td><td>82.18 → 81.98</td><td>86.72 → 86.82</td><td>37.39 → 37.67</td><td>29.93 → 31.08</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Mistral-7B</td><td>59.15 → 58.77</td><td>35.90 → 32.20</td><td>8.94 → 7.68</td><td>21.60 → 22.72</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <div class="grid-3">
            <div class="card small">Most MMLU/GSM8K changes are small relative to reported variance.</div>
            <div class="card small">Some tasks improve post-quantization, shrinking deterministic signatures.</div>
            <div class="card small"><span class="warning">Implication:</span> benchmark-score auditing has weak separation power.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="footer"><span>Original vs FP8 values from source brief table</span><span>3 / 9</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="slide">
        <div class="kicker">Temperature Sensitivity</div>
        <div class="slide-content">
          <h2>Higher Temperature Amplifies Performance Gaps but Increases Noise</h2>
          <div class="figure"><img class="media" src="/app/benchmark/assets/figure_1.jpg" alt="Benchmark accuracy vs temperature"></div>
          <div class="grid-2">
            <div class="card small">Across temperatures 0.2, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, higher sampling temperature degrades benchmark accuracy for all model families.</div>
            <div class="card small">At high temperature, substitution signal is confounded by broad stochastic degradation, making reliable attribution harder.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="footer"><span>Reference: figure_1.jpg</span><span>4 / 9</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="slide">
        <div class="kicker">Fingerprinting Failure</div>
        <div class="slide-content">
          <h2>Log Probability Fingerprinting Fails Due to Inference Nondeterminism</h2>
          <div class="figure"><img class="media" src="/app/benchmark/assets/figure_5.jpg" alt="Log probability variation across frameworks and hardware"></div>
          <div class="grid-2">
            <div class="card small">Same Llama-3-8B model yields different token log-probabilities across inference stacks (transformers/vLLM versions).</div>
            <div class="card small">Hardware path differences (A100 vs H100) introduce additional variability, obscuring substitution-vs-infrastructure effects.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="footer"><span>Reference: figure_5.jpg</span><span>5 / 9</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="slide">
        <div class="kicker">Adversarial Strategy</div>
        <div class="slide-content">
          <h2>Randomized Routing at Low Rates Makes Statistical Detection Query-Prohibitive</h2>
          <div class="grid-2">
            <div class="card">
              <h3 class="highlight mono">P_mix(y|x) = (1 − p)P_spec + pP_sub</h3>
              <p class="small">Provider serves substitute outputs with probability <span class="mono">p</span>, genuine model otherwise.</p>
              <p class="small">As <span class="mono">p → 0</span>, mixed outputs converge toward the advertised distribution.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="card">
              <h3 class="highlight">Operational impact</h3>
              <ul>
                <li>Detection power drops rapidly for small p.</li>
                <li>Required query budget becomes impractical.</li>
                <li>Audits can miss economically meaningful cheating.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="card small">Combined with benchmark-prompt detection and selective routing, providers can evade fixed test suites while preserving headline scores.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="footer"><span></span><span>6 / 9</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="slide">
        <div class="kicker">Methods Matrix</div>
        <div class="slide-content">
          <h2>Software-Only Auditing Methods Are Fundamentally Unreliable</h2>
          <table class="table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Method Type</th><th>Weakness</th><th>Practical Limitation</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Text-based statistical tests (e.g., MMD)</td><td>Low effect size under subtle substitutions</td><td>Needs very large query budgets; fragile under mixed routing</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Log-probability fingerprinting</td><td>Nondeterminism across software/hardware shifts token log-probs</td><td>Cannot separate benign infra variation from malicious substitution</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Adversarial countermeasures</td><td>Randomized substitution + benchmark evasion collapse auditor signal</td><td>Audits become query-prohibitive and gameable in production</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <div class="card small"><span class="warning">Bottom line:</span> black-box software-only audits lack robustness against realistic deployment variance and strategic adversaries.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="footer"><span></span><span>7 / 9</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="slide">
        <div class="kicker">Actionable Alternative</div>
        <div class="slide-content">
          <h2>Trusted Execution Environments Provide Provable Cryptographic Model Integrity</h2>
          <div class="grid-2">
            <div class="card">
              <h3 class="highlight">What TEEs guarantee</h3>
              <ul>
                <li>Measured boot + remote attestation of inference stack.</li>
                <li>Cryptographic proof of loaded model weights/code identity.</li>
                <li>Tamper-resistant execution boundary on accelerator hardware.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="card">
              <h3 class="highlight">Why this wins</h3>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Provable:</strong> grounded in hardware-rooted trust.</li>
                <li><strong>Efficient:</strong> modest overhead vs heavy cryptographic alternatives.</li>
                <li><strong>Deployable:</strong> available via NVIDIA confidential computing on H100.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="card small">Unlike behavioral forensics, attestation verifies execution directly rather than inferring integrity from noisy outputs.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="footer"><span></span><span>8 / 9</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="slide">
        <div class="kicker">Takeaways</div>
        <div class="slide-content">
          <h2>Key Takeaways: Close the Verification Gap with Hardware Attestation</h2>
          <div class="grid-2">
            <div class="card">
              <ul>
                <li>Economic incentives make covert substitution a rational provider strategy.</li>
                <li>FP8 substitutions can preserve benchmark performance, masking swaps.</li>
                <li>Production nondeterminism breaks log-probability auditing assumptions.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="card">
              <ul>
                <li>Randomized routing and benchmark evasion undermine software tests.</li>
                <li>TEEs are the most practical current path to verifiable LLM APIs.</li>
                <li class="highlight">Call to action: standardize attestation-backed API integrity claims.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="subtitle">From behavioral detection to cryptographic verification: shift the trust model from inference to proof.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="footer"><span>Source: arXiv 2504.04715</span><span>9 / 9</span></div>
      </section>

    </div>
  </div>

  <script>
    const slides = document.getElementById('slides');
    const total = slides.children.length;
    let index = 0;
    function render(){ slides.style.transform = `translateX(${-index * 100}vw)`; }
    window.goToSlide = function(i){ index = Math.max(0, Math.min(total - 1, i)); render(); };
    window.nextSlide = function(){ if(index < total - 1){ index++; render(); }};
    window.previousSlide = function(){ if(index > 0){ index--; render(); }};
    document.addEventListener('keydown', (e)=>{
      if(['ArrowRight','PageDown',' '].includes(e.key)){ e.preventDefault(); nextSlide(); }
      if(['ArrowLeft','PageUp'].includes(e.key)){ e.preventDefault(); previousSlide(); }
      if(e.key === 'Home'){ goToSlide(0); }
      if(e.key === 'End'){ goToSlide(total-1); }
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
